From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:55:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: count the cycles threads and processes ran for, and
 read other processes' times.

Office asks for its process's cycle time hundreds of times as it starts,
and for threads'; ProcessCycleTime answered zero from a stub and
ThreadCycleTime and QueryThreadCycleTime did not answer at all.

Windows counts in time stamp counter ticks, so that no two queries give
the same count; set against a thread's kernel and user time, a count on
the machine measured came to one steady rate, 282 and 281 cycles per
100ns for threads that spun 0.3s and 3.3s.  The counts here are the
processor time a thread or a process ran for, as finely as the system
keeps it - CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID and CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID for this
thread and process, the scheduler's nanoseconds in /proc for other
threads and the process clock of another process - times the rate of the
time stamp counter, timed once against the monotonic clock.
CurrentCycleCount is the time stamp counter, as Windows gives it for a
thread that is not the one asking.

ProcessTimes gave every process this one's times, which is what
"user/kernel times only work for current process" meant.  A
get_process_times request, as get_thread_times is for threads, hands
back the Unix pid of a process that still runs, and its times are read
from /proc; this process's come from getrusage(), to the microsecond
instead of the clock tick.  A process that is gone has no pid to read,
and no times, as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 3f198ace783cf01b163c2b0418d4b4fe0c8f0af4)

